1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,020
In the early 2000s, education delegations
from around the world started

2
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:06,471
flying to Helsinki.

3
00:00:06,491 --> 00:00:11,671
Finland's national education agency alone
has hosted more than sixteen thousand

4
00:00:11,691 --> 00:00:12,369
visitors.

5
00:00:12,389 --> 00:00:16,849
Today it charges one thousand four hundred
and eighty-five euros for a single

6
00:00:16,869 --> 00:00:17,766
school visit.

7
00:00:18,016 --> 00:00:19,638
They came to see a miracle.

8
00:00:19,688 --> 00:00:25,268
In 2006, Finnish fifteen-year-olds scored
548 in maths,

9
00:00:25,288 --> 00:00:27,542
on the world's largest school test.

10
00:00:27,792 --> 00:00:28,785
Then it fell.

11
00:00:28,805 --> 00:00:31,761
Every round since has come in lower than
the last.

12
00:00:31,781 --> 00:00:34,641
The latest score: 469.

13
00:00:34,891 --> 00:00:36,555
That's 79 points.

14
00:00:36,605 --> 00:00:40,705
By the OECD's own yardstick, roughly three
years of learning,

15
00:00:40,725 --> 00:00:41,238
gone.

16
00:00:41,488 --> 00:00:45,340
The usual suspects are phones, the
pandemic and immigration.

17
00:00:45,360 --> 00:00:46,951
None of them explains it.

18
00:00:46,971 --> 00:00:48,988
The real answer is stranger.

19
00:00:49,008 --> 00:00:51,548
The world copied Finland for the wrong
reasons,

20
00:00:51,568 --> 00:00:54,726
and Finland may have made the same mistake
about itself.

21
00:00:56,176 --> 00:00:59,227
The test is PISA, run by the OECD.

22
00:00:59,277 --> 00:01:03,257
Roughly every three years, it measures
fifteen-year-olds in reading,

23
00:01:03,277 --> 00:01:04,571
maths and science.

24
00:01:04,661 --> 00:01:08,934
The 2025 round covered ninety-one
countries and economies.

25
00:01:09,184 --> 00:01:11,607
It doesn't test what students memorised.

26
00:01:11,627 --> 00:01:15,244
It tests whether they can use it on
problems they've never seen.

27
00:01:15,264 --> 00:01:17,921
A bad result can end a minister's career.

28
00:01:17,941 --> 00:01:20,758
A good one can launch a consulting
industry.

29
00:01:22,208 --> 00:01:25,628
The first results came out in December
2001,

30
00:01:25,648 --> 00:01:28,471
and Finland was top of the world in
reading.

31
00:01:28,491 --> 00:01:33,111
By 2006 it was scoring 563 in science,

32
00:01:33,131 --> 00:01:38,134
548 in maths and 547 in reading.

33
00:01:38,384 --> 00:01:41,724
Visitors found a system that seemed to
break every rule.

34
00:01:41,744 --> 00:01:43,249
School starts at seven.

35
00:01:43,269 --> 00:01:46,567
No national tests until the end of upper
secondary.

36
00:01:46,587 --> 00:01:48,135
No school inspectors.

37
00:01:48,155 --> 00:01:51,419
And every primary teacher holds a master's
degree.

38
00:01:51,669 --> 00:01:52,983
All of that was true.

39
00:01:53,003 --> 00:01:55,297
The mistake was one word: because.

40
00:01:55,317 --> 00:01:57,617
Finland tested less and scored more,

41
00:01:57,637 --> 00:02:01,185
so the world concluded it scored more
because it tested less.

42
00:02:01,515 --> 00:02:04,497
But look at who sat the 2006 test.

43
00:02:04,517 --> 00:02:09,009
Born around 1991, they started school
around 1998.

44
00:02:09,029 --> 00:02:12,929
The famous freedoms had only been written
into the rules in the 1990s,

45
00:02:12,949 --> 00:02:15,969
and many of their teachers had trained in
an older,

46
00:02:15,989 --> 00:02:17,873
far more centralised system.

47
00:02:17,893 --> 00:02:19,655
Rules change in a year.

48
00:02:19,675 --> 00:02:21,915
Classrooms change over decades.

49
00:02:23,365 --> 00:02:25,361
Here is the whole maths record.

50
00:02:25,381 --> 00:02:30,471
A small climb to the 2006 peak, then a
lower score in every round since.

51
00:02:30,491 --> 00:02:31,911
This isn't a blip.

52
00:02:31,931 --> 00:02:34,918
It's a trend that has run for almost
twenty years.

53
00:02:35,168 --> 00:02:41,028
Reading followed the same path, from 547
down to 474,

54
00:02:41,048 --> 00:02:43,297
now joint seventeenth with Italy.

55
00:02:43,387 --> 00:02:49,164
Science held up best, but still slid from
563 to 504.

56
00:02:49,184 --> 00:02:52,951
Finland is still above the OECD average in
all three.

57
00:02:52,971 --> 00:02:54,934
In maths, by just six points.

58
00:02:55,224 --> 00:02:56,860
Part of this is global.

59
00:02:56,880 --> 00:03:03,020
Across the OECD, reading fell 28 points
and maths 22 between 2015

60
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:04,444
and 2025.

61
00:03:04,464 --> 00:03:07,985
But Finland fell further, and the damage
shows at both ends.

62
00:03:08,235 --> 00:03:12,415
In the early 2000s, about seven per cent
of Finnish students were below

63
00:03:12,435 --> 00:03:13,964
the baseline in maths.

64
00:03:13,984 --> 00:03:16,321
By 2022, one in four.

65
00:03:16,341 --> 00:03:17,686
The top shrank too.

66
00:03:17,736 --> 00:03:22,465
In 2003, more than twenty-three per cent
reached the highest levels.

67
00:03:22,485 --> 00:03:24,982
In 2025, seven per cent.

68
00:03:25,232 --> 00:03:26,247
Now the neighbour.

69
00:03:26,267 --> 00:03:28,887
Estonia sits just across the Gulf of
Finland,

70
00:03:28,907 --> 00:03:32,070
and its language is a close relative of
Finnish.

71
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:36,807
In 2006, it trailed Finland in maths by
thirty-three points.

72
00:03:36,827 --> 00:03:38,993
By 2012, the two were level.

73
00:03:39,013 --> 00:03:42,313
In 2025, Estonia led by thirty-nine,

74
00:03:42,333 --> 00:03:45,009
and it was ahead in reading and science
too.

75
00:03:45,259 --> 00:03:49,078
And the top of the OECD table in 2025?

76
00:03:49,128 --> 00:03:52,428
Japan, first in all three subjects for the
first time.

77
00:03:52,448 --> 00:03:54,935
So should everyone just copy East Asia?

78
00:03:54,955 --> 00:03:56,150
Hold that thought.

79
00:03:57,600 --> 00:03:59,873
Six explanations get the blame.

80
00:03:59,893 --> 00:04:02,033
A good one has to explain three things:

81
00:04:02,053 --> 00:04:05,033
why Finland fell, why it fell further than
most,

82
00:04:05,053 --> 00:04:08,001
and why it started after 2006.

83
00:04:08,251 --> 00:04:10,268
Suspect one: immigration.

84
00:04:10,288 --> 00:04:14,076
Scores fell among students with and
without a migrant background.

85
00:04:14,096 --> 00:04:18,516
In maths, immigrant-background students
held roughly steady since 2012,

86
00:04:18,536 --> 00:04:20,444
and the gap actually narrowed.

87
00:04:20,464 --> 00:04:21,787
Not the main cause.

88
00:04:22,077 --> 00:04:24,695
Suspect two: phones and the pandemic.

89
00:04:24,715 --> 00:04:29,847
The OECD links heavy leisure use of
devices at school to lower scores.

90
00:04:29,867 --> 00:04:32,007
But that's a correlation, it's global,

91
00:04:32,027 --> 00:04:36,300
and Finland's slide began more than a
decade before COVID-19.

92
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:38,347
An accelerant, not the origin.

93
00:04:38,597 --> 00:04:40,337
Suspect three: reading.

94
00:04:40,357 --> 00:04:45,017
Finland's own education agency says
reading used to be a popular pastime

95
00:04:45,037 --> 00:04:46,513
that held results up.

96
00:04:46,533 --> 00:04:51,953
Between 2000 and 2009, the share of
Finnish students reading for enjoyment

97
00:04:51,973 --> 00:04:55,100
fell faster than the OECD average.

98
00:04:55,120 --> 00:04:58,183
And every maths question is also a reading
test.

99
00:04:58,203 --> 00:04:59,889
A likely contributor.

100
00:05:00,179 --> 00:05:02,647
Suspect four: student-led learning.

101
00:05:02,667 --> 00:05:06,887
A new curriculum built around phenomenon-
based learning reached schools

102
00:05:06,907 --> 00:05:08,076
in 2016.

103
00:05:08,096 --> 00:05:10,711
But scores had already been falling for a
decade.

104
00:05:10,731 --> 00:05:12,246
Possible, not proven.

105
00:05:12,536 --> 00:05:14,620
Suspect five: inequality.

106
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:17,020
More low performers, fewer top performers,

107
00:05:17,040 --> 00:05:18,001
wider gaps.

108
00:05:18,021 --> 00:05:18,982
All true.

109
00:05:19,032 --> 00:05:20,796
But that describes the damage.

110
00:05:20,816 --> 00:05:22,267
It doesn't explain it.

111
00:05:22,557 --> 00:05:24,449
Suspect six: the legacy.

112
00:05:24,469 --> 00:05:29,649
In 2015, the economist Gabriel Heller
Sahlgren argued that Finland's rise

113
00:05:29,669 --> 00:05:32,689
began before its celebrated reforms took
effect,

114
00:05:32,709 --> 00:05:35,724
and its decline began soon after they did.

115
00:05:35,744 --> 00:05:38,364
The miracle, he said, came from something
older:

116
00:05:38,384 --> 00:05:41,206
teacher-led classrooms and deep cultural
roots.

117
00:05:41,456 --> 00:05:44,076
Now shift every maths score back eight
years,

118
00:05:44,096 --> 00:05:46,268
to when those students started school.

119
00:05:46,288 --> 00:05:50,673
The best results belong to children who
started in the late 1990s.

120
00:05:50,693 --> 00:05:54,844
The decline begins with those who started
in the early 2000s.

121
00:05:54,864 --> 00:05:56,561
That doesn't prove the theory.

122
00:05:56,581 --> 00:05:59,462
But no other suspect fits the timing as
well.

123
00:06:00,912 --> 00:06:02,844
So is East Asia the answer?

124
00:06:02,864 --> 00:06:08,044
Japan tops the OECD, yet only sixty-three
per cent of its students say they're

125
00:06:08,064 --> 00:06:12,924
curious about many things, against
seventy-three across the OECD.

126
00:06:12,944 --> 00:06:16,647
Forty-eight per cent say they push harder
when work gets difficult.

127
00:06:16,667 --> 00:06:18,971
The OECD average is sixty.

128
00:06:19,221 --> 00:06:23,921
Critics like the education scholar Yong
Zhao argue that test-driven systems

129
00:06:23,941 --> 00:06:27,169
can buy high scores at the cost of
creativity.

130
00:06:27,189 --> 00:06:30,390
One test on one day is not a verdict on a
nation.

131
00:06:30,640 --> 00:06:34,023
And Finland is still above average in
every subject.

132
00:06:34,043 --> 00:06:38,623
Sliding from the top of the reading table
to joint seventeenth is a crisis

133
00:06:38,643 --> 00:06:39,729
in Helsinki.

134
00:06:39,749 --> 00:06:42,417
In most capitals, it would be a press
release.

135
00:06:43,867 --> 00:06:45,756
Finland's success was real.

136
00:06:45,776 --> 00:06:47,596
But the world copied what it could see:

137
00:06:47,616 --> 00:06:50,524
the late start, the missing tests, the
freedom.

138
00:06:50,544 --> 00:06:54,244
It missed what it couldn't see: a
generation that read for pleasure,

139
00:06:54,264 --> 00:06:56,743
and teachers who taught the basics
directly.

140
00:06:56,763 --> 00:07:00,390
Those couldn't be exported, and at home
they quietly faded.

141
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:03,820
So the next time a headline announces an
education miracle,

142
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:05,345
ask three questions.

143
00:07:05,365 --> 00:07:07,681
When were these students actually taught?

144
00:07:07,701 --> 00:07:09,868
What changed before the scores did?

145
00:07:09,888 --> 00:07:11,787
And what's happening at the bottom?

146
00:07:13,237 --> 00:07:16,215
Finland didn't fall because it stopped
being Finland.

147
00:07:16,235 --> 00:07:19,841
It fell, at least partly, because it
started believing the brochure.

148
00:07:20,091 --> 00:07:23,505
What did your school get right that no
ranking would ever measure?

